an class=3D"post_mention">1. </span><span class=3D"post_mention">I chose to=
 use the post from donewithdogs that displays a set of instructions that co=
ntains no words but only illustrations. This particular tweet struck a chor=
d with me because, while I generally prefer instructions that contain graph=
ics, it is quite ineffective to merely have pictures without written direct=
ions on what to do. It is best to include pictures to visually represent th=
e desired outcome and words to explain the steps that must be followed step=
-by-step. Another problem I have with this specific set of instructions is =
that they mark both the steps and the pieces with letters, which makes them=
 first unclear. </span></p>
<p><span class=3D"post_mention">2.Based on this post and numerous others, I=
've discovered that having well-structured and unambiguous instructions is =
the greatest method to assist customers. In order to prevent confusion, wri=
ting should first be consistent and employ straightforward language.&nbsp;<=
br>Accurate orientation, scale, and part arrangement should then be demonst=
rated in the pictures. To prevent confusion, parts and steps should then be=
 identified. Parts should be labeled with letters, and steps should be labe=
led with numbers. Finally, be sure to engage a professional translation if =
the instructions will be translated into a different language.&nbsp;</span>=
