gin-left: 0.375rem;"><span class=3D"user_content enhanced jqueryUIWidgetize=
d" data-resource-type=3D"discussion_topic.reply" data-resource-id=3D"230013=
7"><p><span>I came across a tweet from the @uselessassist account that real=
ly resonated with me: =E2=80=9CTwitter has </span><span>zero customer servi=
ce</span><span>=E2=80=A6 I cannot contact a single person for assistance=E2=
=80=A6 Everything just keeps forwarding to the completely useless and frust=
rating ticket system.=E2=80=9D What makes this tweet so frustrating is the =
complete absence of human support. The user is caught in an endless loop of=
 automated ticket systems with no way to speak to an actual person or even =
understand why their issue occurred. There=E2=80=99s a clear lack of transp=
arency, no sense of progress, and no control over a situation that may affe=
ct something as personal and important as an account tied to their identity=
 or data.</span></p>
<p><span>This tweet hit home because I=E2=80=99ve experienced the same thin=
g=E2=80=94being locked out of an account, submitting tickets that disappear=
 into a void, and feeling completely powerless. The sense of being stuck, h=
elpless, and ignored is incredibly frustrating. It reminds me of the import=
ance of designing user help that actually </span><span>helps</span><span>. =
From this, I take away several key lessons for writing better user help: al=
ways offer a real way to reach a human, be transparent about what=E2=80=99s=
 going on and why, empower users to take clear, meaningful actions, and avo=
id directing them through endless support loops. Good support isn=E2=80=99t=
 just about solving problems=E2=80=94it=E2=80=99s about reassuring users th=
at they=E2=80=99re seen, heard, and respected.</span></p></span></div><span=
