d" data-resource-type=3D"discussion_topic.reply" data-resource-id=3D"229977=
0"><p>Tweet:&nbsp;</p>
<p>"I ask the chatbot to connect me to a human. It responds, 'Please provid=
e more details.' I say 'Useless!' and it can't transfer me=E2=80=94just loo=
ps forever."&nbsp;</p>
<p>I noticed a tweet in which one person chats with a bot, requesting to co=
nnect him or her with a human, but the bot does not assist in any way, but =
asks to add more information. Even when the user typed in Useless!, it fail=
ed to transfer him/her and it simply looped endlessly. I can truly relate t=
o that, personally, especially when I was trying to get assistance and got =
overlooked or looped into an infinite cycle with a robot that cannot compre=
hend frustration.</p>
<p>It is so frustrating because one cannot control it. When you are already=
 stressed and the bot does not want to involve the person in charge, then t=
here is a feeling of no one listening. I remember this tweet because it rev=
eals the danger of technology in the aspect of causing distracting obstruct=
ion of genuine assistance.</p>
<p>The thing I would do to produce better user help is to ensure that there=
 is always a clear, misunderstanding-free path to communicating with a huma=
n being. The help system would also be programmed to detect tokens of frust=
ration, say angry words or instances of repetition, and respond empathetica=
lly. Best of all, I would make the process fast and assisting, instead of c=
omplex and mechanical. The best help to users should not burrow itself in a=
 loop but feel heard and helped.</p>
